  "executive_briefing": "The corporate and international tax policy landscape in mid-2026 is defined by the hardening of multi-jurisdictional compliance and the escalation of protectionist fiscal instruments. The OECD's Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax is no longer a prospective reform but a primary operational reality for MNEs, with enforcement mechanisms now fully integrated into the tax reporting cycles of major economies. Trade policy has entered a high-volatility phase; the U.S. and other major powers are increasingly utilizing targeted tariffs\u2014moving beyond broad strokes to sector-specific interventions in metals and pharmaceuticals. Simultaneously, the digitization of tax administration is reaching maturity, with jurisdictions like Singapore utilizing Peppol-based e-invoicing and real-time validation to institutionalize Continuous Transaction Controls (CTCs) as the new standard for GST compliance. ASEAN remains a critical focal point, balancing standard corporate tax competitiveness with specialized economic zones to maintain FDI attractiveness against global tax floors.",
